  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved decent employment opportunities for youth through enterpreneurship development and support services and skill training, including School to Work Transition surveys and tools.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">It is highlighted that there is a mismatch between what students entering the workforce have been taught in school and the skills necessary for the marketplace, especially in terms of skills for the future. The need for digital skills gained pre-eminence during the COVID-19 pandemic, particularly during the lockdown when the work from home modality became the most preferred form of work. While there is a lot of positive progress, challenges still remain and one of them is to translate these changes to an optimal matching of labour supply to demand. The 2023 UN Common Country Assessment (CCA) Update highlights youth unemployment as a pressing issue in Samoa, with young people being left behind in the post-COVID-19 recovery driven by construction and tourism. ILO supports the constituents in conducting research on the underlying barriers for women in the labour market; provision of PSET training, upskilling and re-skilling opportunities targeting women and youth including persons with disabilities &#x2013; building on the research on women employability and, tailoring employment services specifically for women. Improved decent employment opportunities for youth through entreprenuership development and support services and skills training, including School to Work Transition surveys and tools. Therefore the constituents will work together with ILO to increase capacity of production, analysis and use of sex disaggregated labour market statistics to promote and support access of young women and men including persons with disabilities to decent employment opportunities.</narrative>
  </description>
